{% set local_problem %}
<p>Toney properties face drainage challenges that suburban homes don't. Without municipal storm drains, water has to go somewhere on your property. Combined with Madison County's clay soil — which doesn't absorb water well — surface drainage often just moves the problem around.</p>

<p>Many rural Toney properties have grading issues, low spots where water collects, or foundations built before modern drainage standards. Surface extensions help a little, but for serious water problems, you need to get water far enough away that it can't return.</p>

<p>Underground drains are the permanent solution. They route roof runoff through buried pipes, depositing it at the edge of your property or into a dry well — far from anything you need to protect.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water through a sealed channel to a discharge point far from your home. Water exits through a pop-up emitter, into a dry well, or at the property edge.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or PVC pipe, properly sloped to ensure water flows away rather than pooling in the pipe. The system is designed for your specific property — routing around obstacles, matching your grading, and depositing water where it makes sense.</p>

<p>For rural Toney properties, underground drains often make more sense than elaborate surface extensions. You have the space for proper drainage — underground systems use that space effectively.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage on Toney properties for over 20 years. We understand rural drainage challenges: no city storm drains to connect to, varied terrain, and the need for solutions that work with your property rather than against it.</p>

<p>Our systems are built to last decades. Quality materials, proper installation, and thoughtful design mean your drainage problem gets solved permanently.</p>
{% endset %}

